    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Sarina Deb's capstone project served as a culmination of her work in the class History 255D: Racial Identity in the American Imagination, a course which focused on exploring racial identity through a variety of contemporary and historic lenses. Her final paper centered the history and implications of adultification bias on Black girlhood, tracing this phenomenon back to the \"Jezebel\" stereotype of Black girls and women during the enslavement period, the Jim Crow era, and the post-civil rights movement. Motivated by her interest in criminal justice reform, Sarina's paper evaluates how these longstanding stereotypes and biases have culminated in high rates of sexual violence and sex trafficking for Black girls, school pushout, and the subsequent funneling of these girls into prisons and jails, which she argues exacerbates a human rights crisis for a historically marginalized population.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Deb, S. (2024). The “Jezebel” to Prison Pipeline: A Historical Analysis of the Impact of Adultification Bias on Black Girlhood . Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/wb582kz1323. https://doi.org/10.25740/wb582kz1323.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
